    topic: 'FRENCH BASICS',
    subtopic: 'Basics of French',
    summary_60s: 'French, known for its elegance, has a rich history that makes it fascinating for students. Its origins trace back to the Roman Empire\'s conquest of Gaul (modern-day France) between 58 BCE and 51 BCE. The Romans introduced Vulgar Latin , which replaced the native Celtic language, ',
    key_formulas: '',
    pro_tips_95: 'Official JAMB & WAEC Syllabus Concept: Focus on core definitions, formulas, and past questions.',
    syllabus_objectives: 'Candidates should be able to master the fundamental principles of Basics of French in line with the official syllabus.',
    updated_at: 'JAMB • WAEC Official Syllabus',
    content: `<p>French, known for its elegance, has a rich history that makes it fascinating for students. Its origins trace back to the Roman Empire's conquest of Gaul (modern-day France) between 58 BCE and 51 BCE.</p><p>The Romans introduced <em>Vulgar Latin</em>, which replaced the native Celtic language, Gaulish, especially in southern regions. This shift laid the foundation for the French language.</p><p>In the 4th century, Germanic tribes (the Franks) settled in Gaul. After the fall of the Roman Empire, they influenced early French. King Clovis unified the Franks and adopted Gallo-Roman, blending elements that shaped the language.</p><p>A key milestone was the <em>Oaths of Strasbourg</em> in 842, where Proto-French appeared in writing, marking its evolution from Vulgar Latin.</p><h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>Old French and Middle French</strong></h2><p>Between the 10th and 13th centuries, Old French developed. Latin remained the language of religion and law, but French gradually gained prominence.</p><p>By the 14th and 15th centuries, Middle French emerged, bringing changes like simplified grammar and altered word order. The invention of the printing press and the Ordinance of Villers-Cotterêts in 1539 helped standardize French, making it the official language of laws.</p><h2 style="text-align:center"><strong>French as a Global Language</strong></h2><p>French became popular among the elite and spread as a <em>lingua franca</em> across Europe and beyond, even though many in France didn't speak it fluently during the French Revolution.</p><p>Today, French is an official language in 28 countries and spoken on every continent. It is the second most geographically widespread language after English, valued for its role in culture, diplomacy, and history.</p><h2><strong>French Words and Meaning</strong></h2><ol><li>Bonjour (Hello).</li><li>Merci (Thank you).</li><li>Maison (House).</li><li>Livre (Book).</li><li>Chien (Dog).</li><li>Chat (Cat).</li><li>Soleil (Sun).</li><li>Lune (Moon).</li><li>Chapeau (Hat).</li><li>Voiture (Car).</li><li>Vélo (Bicycle).</li><li>Rue (Street).</li><li>Pluie (Rain).</li><li>Neige (Snow).</li><li>École (School).</li><li>Stylo (Pen).</li><li>Pomme (Apple).</li><li>Pain (Bread).</li><li>Eau (Water).</li><li>Thé (Tea).</li><li>Café (Coffee).</li><li>Lait (Milk).</li><li>Ami (Friend).</li><li>Famille (Family).</li><li>Heureux (Happy).</li><li>Triste (Sad).</li><li>Grand (Tall/Big).</li><li>Petit (Small).</li><li>Froid (Cold).</li><li>Chaud (Hot).</li><li>Rapide (Fast).</li><li>Lent (Slow).</li><li>Facile (Easy).</li><li>Difficile (Difficult).</li><li>Léger (Light).</li><li>Lourd (Heavy).</li><li>Beau (Beautiful).</li><li>Laide (Ugly).</li><li>Jeune (Young).</li><li>Vieux (Old).</li><li>Nouveau (New).</li><li>Ancien (Ancient/Old).</li><li>Riche (Rich).</li><li>Pauvre (Poor).</li><li>Fort (Strong).</li><li>Faible (Weak).</li><li>Intelligent (Intelligent).</li><li>Stupide (Stupid).</li><li>Drôle (Funny).</li><li>Ennuyeux (Boring).</li></ol><h2><strong>Speech Acts</strong></h2><p>Characteristics: A speech act is an utterance that serves a function in communication. We perform speech acts when we offer an apology, greeting, request, complaint, invitation, compliment/admiration, or refusal. A speech act might contain just one word, as in Desolé! - "Sorry!" to perform an apology, or several words or sentences:</p><p>"Je suis désolé d'avoir oublié ton anniversaire. Je l'ai juste laissé échapper de mon esprit."</p><p><em>"I’m sorry I forgot your birthday. I just let it slip my mind."</em></p><p>Speech acts include real-life interactions and require not only knowledge of the language but also appropriate use of that language within a given culture.</p><h1 style="text-align:center"><strong>Every Day French</strong></h1><h2>Les Salutations - Greetings</h2><p>Bonjour Eric, ça va ?</p><p><em>Hi, Eric. How are things going?</em></p><p><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="height:48px; width:100px"/></p><h2>Demander - Request</h2><p>Pourriez-vous me passer la purée de pommes de terre, s'il vous plaît ?</p><p><em>Could you pass me the mashed potatoes, please?</em></p><h2>Conseiller - To Advise</h2><p>Pour affronter l'hiver,tu dois manger de la soupe.</p><p><em>To face the winter, you have to eat soup.</em></p><p>Pour aller à la gare, vous devez prendre la prochaine route à droite.</p><p><em>To go to the train station, you have to take the next road on the right.</em></p><p><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="height:100px; width:99px"/></p><h2>Admirer - Express love or admiration</h2><p>Tu es très beau.</p><p><em>You are very handsome</em></p><p>Votre sourire est doux.</p><p><em>Your smile is very gentle.</em></p><h2>Espérer - Hope</h2><p>Je te souhaite une bonne journée !</p><p><em>I hope you have a great day! </em></p><p>J'espère qu'il va arrêter de pleuvoir.</p><p><em>I hope it stops raining.</em></p><h2>Interroger - Ask questions</h2><p>Qu’est-ce que tu vas voir ?</p><p><em>What are you going to see?</em></p><p>Est-ce que je peux venir avec toi ?</p><p><em>Can you come with us?</em></p><h2>Regretter - Regret/mistake,to be sorry</h2><p>Je regrette de vous téléphoner si tard.</p><p><em>I am sorry to call you this late.</em></p><p>Je suis au regret de vous informer que je ne peux pas aller à la conférence.</p><p><em>I regret to inform you that I can’t go to the conference.</em></p><p><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="height:179px; width:180px"/></p><h2>Reprocher - Reproach / Blame</h2><p>Tu aurais dû venir plus tôt.</p><p><em>You should have come earlier.</em></p><p>Il aurait fallu le lui dire : il aurait pu peut-être nous aider.</p><p><em>Someone should have told him: he might have helped us.</em></p><p><img src="data:image/jpeg;base64," style="height:121px; width:180px"/></p><h2>Proposer - To propose or invite</h2><p>Est-ce que tu veux dîner avec moi ?</p><p><em>Do you want to have dinner with me?</em></p><p>Est-ce que vous voulez jouer au tennis avec nous ?</p><p><em>Do you want to play tennis with us?</em></p><h2>Refuser - To Refuse</h2><p>Non, désolée, je ne peux pas dîner avec toi ce soir. J’ai déjà des projets.</p><p><em>No, sorry, I can't have dinner with you tonight. I already have plans.</em></p><h2>S’accorder - To agree</h2><p>Oui, d’accord</p><p><em>Yes, OK, I agree</em></p><p>Je veux bien.</p><p><em>OK, I’d love to</em>, meaning YES, I accept.</p><p>Some other expressions you can use include;</p><p>OK, D’accord, Pas de problème, Pas de soucis, Ça marche, Ça roule, Pas grave, Bien / Bon &amp; C’est bon</p><p><strong>References:</strong></p><p>The FlashLearners <strong> Class Notes</strong> are crafted and meticulously refined by a team of over 25 specialists, ensuring adherence to all exam guidelines.</p><p>This comprehensive material distills insights from secondary school scheme, recommended UTME and SSCE Textbooks, 100L admission exam standard, and an extensive array of over 800 digital references.</p><p>Encompassing 26 subjects, our content is a testament to the evolving standards of education and knowledge. While the myriad of individual sources is extensive, we wholeheartedly recognize and appreciate their collective influence on our academic offerings.</p>`
